WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:08.200
Now that we've looked at the basic parts of a worm bin, let's put them together.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:16.000
As we go, pay attention not only to what we're adding, but why each part matters.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:24.660
A healthy worm bin is a living system. The goal is to create good starting conditions and learn to observe how the system responds.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:37.200
There are plenty of ready-made worm bin kits available, but a simple two-bin stacking system is one of the most economical and approachable ways to get started.

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:46.400
It uses basic materials, doesn't take up much space, and gives us the airflow and drainage we're looking for.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:55.260
Start with your containers. Your worm bin needs airflow, so you'll need ventilation holes near the upper part of the inner bin.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:01:06.380
You'll also want drainage holes in the bottom in case excess moisture collects.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:20.710
If you're using a nested-bin setup like this one, spacers between the containers help create room underneath for drainage while keeping the bins from fitting too tightly together.

00:01:20.710 --> 00:01:32.680
The exact container you use may vary depending on your space. What's important is that it's manageable for you and provides the airflow and drainage the system needs.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:47.900
Next, add about four to six inches of bedding.

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:57.660
Shredded cardboard and paper are inexpensive options, and you may already have them at home. Other dry materials can work as well.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:07.920
You'll also want a small amount of grit, such as finely crushed eggshells or a little soil, which helps support the worms' digestive process.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.680
You don't need every material on this list.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:21.050
Think about the function each material serves and use appropriate materials that are practical for you.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:28.660
Once the bedding is in place, add moisture gradually.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:36.110
You're aiming for bedding that is evenly moist, but not saturated.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.900
Add a little water, mix the bedding, and check it before adding more.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:54.420
This is one of those places where observation matters more than following an exact measurement.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.560
Conditions will vary depending on your bedding, container, and environment.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:10.420
Once the bedding environment is ready, it's time to add your worms.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:22.880
Gently place them onto the bedding and give them some time to move into their new environment.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:30.420
You can add a light layer of bedding over them rather than burying them deeply.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:42.770
Notice that we're adding the worms only after we've established their habitat.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:50.420
We're building the environment first and introducing the living part of the system second.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:57.150
Now you can begin feeding.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:03.820
Worms can process a variety of suitable materials, including many fruit and vegetable scraps,

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:11.180
coffee grounds, crushed eggshells, and some paper-based materials. There are also foods you'll want to avoid, which you can see here.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:21.180
For a new bin, resist the temptation to add everything you've saved. Start with a modest amount of food and observe how quickly it disappears.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:41.180
From here, your most important tool is observation. Check the moisture, notice how quickly food is being consumed, and pay attention to changes in odor.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:51.180
If something seems wrong, make a small adjustment based on what you're observing rather than changing several things at once.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:58.100
If you're using this two-bin system, also remember to check the lower container for liquid that has drained from the worm bin.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:03.480
This liquid is called leachate. Check the lower bin regularly and drain it as needed.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.900
If you want to use the leachate around garden plants, dilute it with approximately ten parts water to one part leachate.
